  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">CO-DRA Gaza Joint Response 2025</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Due to war in Gaza more than 80% of WASH infrastructures are damaged and is facing a severe crisis, the water production has been reduced to 22% of pre-war levels. Many water facilities, especially in Gaza City and Northern Gaza, are non-operational, leaving hundreds of thousands without reliable access to waterIn July 2024, Oxfam published the report &#x201c;Water War Crimes,&#x201d; which indicated that in Gaza City, 100% of municipal desalination plants and seawater desalination plants, 88% of water wells, and 89% of water storage reservoirs have been destroyed or rendered non-operational. Based on the Gaza Rapid WASH assessment report from October 2024, more than half of assessed households (64.2%) reported receiving less than 6 liters per person per day (LPPD) ofdrinking and cooking water,while almost half (46.4%) received less than 9 LPPD of domestic water. The hard-to-reach areas in Northern governorates were the worst affected, receiving as little as 2 LPPD of drinking water and 5.1 LPPD of domestic water.Access to safe water is further hindered by storage and infrastructure issues, including insufficient water points and a lack of containers for storage. Water treatment and quality testing remain significant challenges, as insufficient chlorination and contamination with pathogens, such as E. coli and fecal coliforms, exacerbate health risks for Gaza&#x2019;s residents. The December 2024 Water Quality Surveillance report indicates that out of 306 water samples taken from production, distribution, and user points, 79% were non-compliant for Free Residual Chlorine, and 72.8% of the samples collected for drinking water did not meet agreed standards.A recent ceasefire presents a long-awaited opportunity for millions of displaced people to return to their places of origin after being displaced multiple times over the past 15 months. it also brings significant opportunity,  in restoring WASH services and ensuring access to safe water and to complement other ongoing actions (food, Health, protection, shelter) for Gaza&apos;s residents.</narrative>
  </description>
  <participating-org ref="NL-KVK-27108436" role="4" type="21">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Oxfam Novib has been active in the Occupied Palestinian Territory/Israel (OPT/I) since 1982 and working with Palestinian refugees in Lebanon since the mid 1980s. It has built up a strong network of relationships with local NGOs and counts among its localcounterparts some of the largest and most professional NGOs in various sectors. It has accompanied these NGOs in their development from ad hoc volunteer groups to sizeable professional institutions with national outreach.  At the same time the conceptualisation of the programme has evolved from three country programs in the early 1990s to a program for Palestinians in the Middle East (PME) working withPalestinians in OPT, Israel and Lebanon.

The programme has changed over the years with the establishment of the Palestinian Authority (PA) in the mid-1990s from a focus on support and service provision at a grassroots level and grassroots mobilization, to working towards influencing the establishment of the PA, its lawmaking and supporting building a democratic state # this involved supporting efforts of Palestinian civil society to build the basis for a democratic Palestinian state with room for civil participation, ensuring inclusion of vulnerable groups, refugees and women, and shaping its laws and systems. Oxfam Novib has also supported partners that are working on the provision of humanitarian assistance, response preparedness and conflict transformation. We have also supported partners in the provision of health services, access to quality education and gender justice.</narrative>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Oxfam Novib</narrative>
  </participating-org>
